  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{4x}{{e}^{y/2}}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{1}{4x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{\u2032}={f}^{\u2032}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation",
  "worked_solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & ={(4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)}^{\u2032} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{x} \\\\  & \\ne \\frac{1}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x$ and ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{4}{x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{(4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & =\\frac{4}{x}\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
